      <!-- Section 1: Neuroscience of Aim -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          1. The Neuroscience of Aiming: Saccades, Motor Cortex, and Muscle Memory
        </h2>
        <p>
          Aiming in first-person shooter (FPS) titles is often mistakenly viewed as a simple reflex test. In cognitive neuroscience and kinesiology, aiming is classified as a complex <strong>closed-loop visuo-motor coordination task</strong> involving five distinct neurological structures: the retina, superior colliculus, primary visual cortex (V1), posterior parietal cortex, and the cerebellum.
        </p>
        <p>
          When an enemy target appears on your peripheral display, your visual system initiates a <strong>saccade</strong>—a rapid, ballistic eye movement designed to align the target with your fovea centralis (the retina's highest-acuity central zone). Saccadic latency requires approximately 150ms to 200ms. During the saccade, a biological process known as <em>saccadic suppression</em> briefly suspends visual processing to prevent motion blur. Once foveal fixation is achieved, your posterior parietal cortex computes the spatial delta (the angular distance in pixels) and feeds motor commands through the corticospinal tract to the forearm and intrinsic hand muscles.
        </p>
        <p>
          The popular concept of "muscle memory" is neurologically misnamed: muscle fibers possess no cognitive memory storage. Instead, repetitive aim training drives <strong>cerebellar motor learning</strong> and accelerates <strong>axon myelination</strong>. With dedicated daily drills, the motor cortex transitions from slow feedback-driven corrections to fast, feedforward automated motor primitives, allowing you to flick onto targets before conscious cognitive processing occurs.
        </p>
      </section>

