      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          3. Biomechanics & Ergonomics: Arm vs. Wrist vs. Fingertip Aiming
        </h2>
        <p>
          Elite mouse precision relies on a synchronized kinetic chain. Isolating one joint while locking the others creates mechanical bottlenecks and increases repetitive strain injury (RSI) risks:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
          <li>
            <strong>Arm Aiming (Glenohumeral & Elbow Pivots):</strong> Uses the large deltoid and bicep muscle groups to power broad sweeps, clearing 180° turns on low sensitivities (35cm to 55cm per 360° rotation). Provides maximum rotational stability and reduces wrist fatigue.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Wrist Aiming (Carpal Pivot):</strong> Uses the flexor and extensor carpi radialis muscles to execute medium flicks within a 30° to 45° monitor cone. Offers rapid acceleration but limited mechanical range of motion.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Fingertip Micro-Adjustments (Intrinsic Hand Muscles):</strong> The lumbricals and interossei contract to nudge the mouse sensor 2mm to 5mm for precise vertical headshot adjustments and recoil compensation.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="mt-6 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800">
