      <!-- Section 5: Hardware Optimization -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          5. Hardware Optimization: Polling Rates, Sensors, Mousepads & High Refresh Displays
        </h2>
        <p>
          While cognitive motor training accounts for 80% of aim performance, hardware bottlenecks impose an insurmountable physical ceiling. To eliminate hardware latency artifacts:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
          <li>
            <strong>Sensor Smoothing & Acceleration:</strong> Ensure <em>Enhance Pointer Precision</em> is disabled in Windows Mouse Settings. Modern optical sensors (e.g., PixArt PAW3395 and Focus Pro 3950) deliver 1:1 hardware tracking with zero angle snapping and sub-0.5mm lift-off distances (LOD).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>1000Hz vs 4000Hz / 8000Hz Polling:</strong> Upgrading from 1000Hz (1.0ms update interval) to 4000Hz (0.25ms interval) delivers micro-smooth cursor motion on 240Hz+ monitors. Ensure your CPU has high single-core IPC to handle the 4x interrupt request rate.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Display Refresh Rates & Motion Clarity:</strong> Upgrading from 60Hz (16.6ms frame time) to 240Hz (4.16ms) or 360Hz OLED eliminates perceptual ghosting. Your brain tracks target trajectories with higher foveal fidelity, reducing flick calculation errors by up to 22%.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Mousepad Friction Coefficients:</strong> Choose mousepad surfaces according to your primary game genre. Tactical shooter players benefit from textured cloth control pads (higher dynamic friction to stop flicks), while tracking-heavy arena shooter players benefit from hybrid cordura or glass pads (minimal static friction for effortless direction reversals).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

